### What is the problem?

Given an array of elements and a value K, return the K most frequent elements in any order. For example, in `[1,1,1,2,2,3]` with K=2, the answer is `[1, 2]`.

At the National Bank of India, the dashboard shows the K most common transaction types each day.

### How does the frequency counting work?

```java
Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
for (String txn : transactions) {
    freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
}
```

This is O(n) and creates one entry per distinct element.

### How does the min-heap selection work?

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.*;

public class TopKFrequentHeap {
    public static List<String> topK(String[] transactions, int k) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        PriorityQueue<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> heap =
                new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue));

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            heap.offer(entry);
            if (heap.size() > k) {
                heap.poll();
            }
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            result.add(heap.poll().getKey());
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] txns = {"UPI", "NEFT", "UPI", "IMPS", "UPI", "NEFT", "RTGS"};
        System.out.println("Top 2: " + topK(txns, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
Top 2: [NEFT, UPI]
```

### How does the bucket sort selection work?

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.*;

public class TopKFrequentBucket {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static List<String> topK(String[] transactions, int k) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        List<String>[] buckets = new List[transactions.length + 1];
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            int count = entry.getValue();
            if (buckets[count] == null) buckets[count] = new ArrayList<>();
            buckets[count].add(entry.getKey());
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = buckets.length - 1; i >= 0 && result.size() < k; i--) {
            if (buckets[i] != null) {
                result.addAll(buckets[i]);
            }
        }
        return result.subList(0, k);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] txns = {"UPI", "NEFT", "UPI", "IMPS", "UPI", "NEFT", "RTGS"};
        System.out.println("Top 2 (bucket): " + topK(txns, 2));
    }
}
```

```text
Top 2 (bucket): [UPI, NEFT]
```

### Why is bucket sort linear?

A frequency can be at most n (if every element is the same). So the bucket array has at most n+1 entries. Creating it is O(m), filling it is O(m), and scanning it is O(n). No log factor anywhere.

### How do the two selection approaches compare?

| Approach | Counting | Selection | Total |
|---|---|---|---|
| Min-heap | O(n) | O(m log K) | O(n + m log K) |
| Bucket sort | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Full sort | O(n) | O(m log m) | O(n + m log m) |

### What are common mistakes?

Using a max-heap, which evicts the most frequent element instead of the least frequent.

```java
// Wrong: max-heap evicts the most frequent
new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b.getValue() - a.getValue());

// Correct: min-heap evicts the least frequent
new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue));
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **K equals the number of distinct elements**: return all distinct elements.
- **All elements identical**: the single element is the only answer for K=1.
- **Ties**: the problem says any order, so break ties arbitrarily.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The insight is recognizing that frequencies are bounded by n, which makes bucket sort linear. Both approaches share the identical frequency map; they differ only in selection. Stating this trade-off clearly is the senior signal.

For streaming data, use the heap approach because it processes elements one at a time.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What if K is close to the number of distinct elements? (Bucket sort is better.)
- What if elements stream in? (Use heap, updating the frequency map on each arrival.)
- How do you handle ties? (Problem says any order; if not, add a secondary sort key.)
